INT. ABANDONED FAIRGROUND - CAROUSEL PAVILION - NIGHT

A wrought-iron roof over a silent carousel. Painted horses frozen mid-gallop, lacquer cracked, glass eyes dull. Leaves on the platform. A work lamp throws long shadows.

NELL BRANNOCK, 30s, restorer, overalls, kneels with a screwdriver at the central pole. ARCHIE DOYLE, 60s, the caretaker, stands at the edge of the platform with a torch, jingling keys. TOMOKO HAYASHI, 20s, an assistant, kneels by a toolbox, unscrewing a horse's saddle.

Nell works. The pole creaks. Archie steps back. He keeps one hand on the iron post.

NELL
Done soon.

ARCHIE
Take your time.

NELL
It's three a.m.

ARCHIE
I'm used to it.

Tomoko holds up a brass plate from inside the saddle. Reads it.

TOMOKO
"For Margaret, who loved the white horse."

NELL
Who's Margaret?

Archie says nothing. Looks at the lamp.

TOMOKO
There's a date. Nineteen fifty-nine.

ARCHIE
(too fast)
Put it back.

TOMOKO
Why?

ARCHIE
It's not ours.

Nell glances at him. Her screwdriver stops. Overhead, an iron beam ticks. Tomoko slides the plate back and closes the saddle. Her hands shake.

The carousel moves. A quarter turn. The horses lurch one step, then stop. Nobody has touched it.

Nell stands, very slowly.

NELL
Archie. Is there power to this?

ARCHIE
Disconnected in sixty-one.

The platform shifts again. A calliope note, thin and wrong, from somewhere far inside the machine.

Tomoko backs toward the exit. Archie drops his keys. They ring on the iron.

The white horse turns its head.
